WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.679
Last time we turned a key

2
00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:06.422
into a table index

3
00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.557
and we saw that two keys can

4
00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:10.660
land in the same slot.

5
00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.760
Today's question is simple

6
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.200
when that happens

7
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.040
where does the second key go?

8
00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.180
We must not overwrite the first

9
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.570
and we must not lose either one.

10
00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:24.140
The oldest answer is also the simplest

11
00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.790
separate chaining.

12
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.458
In this video we'll see how a chain

13
00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.459
per slot solves collisions

14
00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.411
how the cost of a lookup depends

15
00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:33.630
on the load factor

16
00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.140
and what can still go wrong.

17
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.050
Instead of holding one entry

18
00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.072
every slot holds a chain

19
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:43.204
a list of all the entries whose

20
00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.320
keys hash to that slot.

21
00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:48.060
To insert, compute the index

22
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.140
and look along that chain.

23
00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.960
If the key is already there

24
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.200
replace its value.

25
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.740
If not

26
00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.790
add a new entry at the end of the chain.

27
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.510
To look up, compute the same index

28
00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.910
and walk along that one chain

29
00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.410
comparing keys.

30
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:05.690
To remove

31
00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.740
find the entry in the chain and take it out.

32
00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.194
We never look at any other slot.

33
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:12.566
Everything about one key

34
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:14.370
happens in one chain.

35
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.400
Let's watch it work.

36
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.151
Seven slots, and the hash of an integer

37
00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:21.140
is the integer itself

38
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:24.150
so the index is the key modulo seven.

39
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.710
Twelve goes to slot five.

40
00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.690
Forty-four goes to slot two.

41
00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.870
Thirteen to slot six.

42
00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:33.700
Eighty-eight to slot four.

43
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:37.040
Twenty-three also belongs in slot two.

44
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.420
It joins the chain behind forty-four

45
00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.370
slot two now holds a chain of length two.

46
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.790
Ninety-four goes to slot three.

47
00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:49.410
Eleven joins eighty-eight in slot four.

48
00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:51.030
Thirty-nine joins them too

49
00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:52.440
a chain of three.

50
00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:55.240
Twenty goes behind thirteen

51
00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.880
and sixteen goes to the end of slot two

52
00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.820
which now has three entries: forty-four

53
00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.430
twenty-three and sixteen.

54
00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:05.090
Ten keys in seven slots.

55
00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.270
The load factor is ten over seven

56
00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:08.890
about one point four three

57
00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:11.420
and the longest chain has three entries.

58
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.710
Now look things up.

59
00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:15.990
Twelve is alone in slot five

60
00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:17.560
one comparison.

61
00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.700
Sixteen is the last in slot two

62
00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.960
we compare with forty-four

63
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.180
then twenty-three, then sixteen.

64
00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:26.470
Three comparisons.

65
00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.010
Nine is not in the table.

66
00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.950
Its slot is two as well

67
00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.850
so we walk the whole chain

68
00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:33.910
three comparisons

69
00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:37.020
and only then can we say it is missing.

70
00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.789
Finding each of the ten keys costs

71
00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.260
seventeen comparisons in all

72
00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:43.850
one point seven on average.

73
00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.440
How long are the chains?

74
00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.859
If the hash function spreads keys evenly

75
00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.395
n keys in m slots give chains

76
00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.620
of average length n over m.

77
00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.790
That ratio is the load factor, alpha.

78
00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.523
A lookup costs one hash, one remainder

79
00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:03.429
and then a walk of about alpha

80
00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:04.973
entries for a missing key

81
00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:07.493
or about one plus alpha over two

82
00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:09.470
for a key that is there.

83
00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:12.050
With alpha a small constant

84
00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:13.580
that is constant time.

85
00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.086
Check it against our example

86
00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.958
one plus one point four three over

87
00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.900
two is about one point seven one.

88
00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.330
We measured one point seven zero.

89
00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.610
But notice the condition

90
00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.890
if the hash function spreads keys evenly.

91
00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:32.510
Hash tables are constant time on average

92
00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:34.200
not always.

93
00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:36.604
In the worst case every key

94
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:38.220
lands in one chain

95
00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.153
and a lookup walks them all.

96
00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:41.795
With the add-the-letters

97
00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:43.193
hash from last time

98
00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.341
seven hundred twenty anagrams meant

99
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.260
a chain of seven hundred twenty.

100
00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.260
With a polynomial hash

101
00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.110
the longest chain was four.

102
00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.274
Two things can go wrong.

103
00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.426
The first is a hash function

104
00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:56.960
that does not spread

105
00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:58.930
you have seen what that does.

106
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.510
The second is a table that is too small.

107
00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:05.430
The load factor grows with every insertion

108
00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.366
and so does the chain length.

109
00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:09.772
Next time we'll look at how a table that

110
00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:12.160
has filled up makes itself bigger.

111
00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:14.660
Chains do have one advantage

112
00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.260
the load factor can go above one

113
00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:18.600
and nothing breaks.

114
00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.910
The cost just grows gently.

115
00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:22.740
To sum up.

116
00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:24.900
Each slot holds a chain

117
00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.890
and every operation touches one chain.

118
00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.562
The expected cost is constant if the hash spreads

119
00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:35.000
keys and the load factor stays small.

120
00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:38.620
Next, a different idea: no chains at all.

121
00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.550
If a slot is taken, look in the next one.

122
00:04:42.177 --> 00:04:44.178
Now let us build the chaining

123
00:04:44.247 --> 00:04:45.777
hash map in Python.

124
00:04:46.357 --> 00:04:48.847
The table is a list of buckets

125
00:04:48.927 --> 00:04:50.911
and every bucket is a list

126
00:04:50.987 --> 00:04:52.827
of key and value pairs.

127
00:04:52.827 --> 00:04:54.477
We start with seven.

128
00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:57.377
There is no built-in dictionary inside.

129
00:04:57.377 --> 00:04:59.157
The chains are plain lists

130
00:04:59.157 --> 00:05:00.947
so you can see all the work.

131
00:05:01.527 --> 00:05:04.737
The helper called find scans one chain.

132
00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:06.937
It returns the position of the key

133
00:05:06.937 --> 00:05:08.557
or minus one

134
00:05:08.557 --> 00:05:11.487
and how many comparisons it made.

135
00:05:11.837 --> 00:05:14.247
Put starts the same way as everything else

136
00:05:14.247 --> 00:05:16.307
hash code, compression

137
00:05:16.307 --> 00:05:18.957
then find in that one bucket.

138
00:05:19.307 --> 00:05:23.317
If find found the key, we replace the value

139
00:05:23.317 --> 00:05:24.657
and return the old one.

140
00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:27.317
Otherwise we append a pair to the chain

141
00:05:27.317 --> 00:05:29.347
and count the new entry.

142
00:05:29.927 --> 00:05:32.597
Run it on the ten keys from the video.

143
00:05:32.597 --> 00:05:34.877
Every put prints its hash

144
00:05:34.877 --> 00:05:37.087
its index and the length of the chain.

145
00:05:37.437 --> 00:05:40.327
Slot two ends with forty-four

146
00:05:40.327 --> 00:05:42.167
twenty-three and sixteen.

147
00:05:42.167 --> 00:05:44.807
Slot four ends with eighty-eight

148
00:05:44.807 --> 00:05:47.127
eleven and thirty-nine.

149
00:05:47.127 --> 00:05:50.087
The load factor is one point four three

150
00:05:50.087 --> 00:05:52.277
and the longest chain is three.

151
00:05:52.627 --> 00:05:54.754
Lookups print how many comparisons

152
00:05:54.817 --> 00:05:56.797
they made: one for twelve

153
00:05:56.797 --> 00:05:59.097
three for sixteen, and three for nine

154
00:05:59.097 --> 00:06:00.101
which is missing.

155
00:06:00.157 --> 00:06:02.487
All ten keys together cost

156
00:06:02.577 --> 00:06:04.347
seventeen comparisons.

157
00:06:04.927 --> 00:06:06.757
How do we know it is correct?

158
00:06:06.757 --> 00:06:10.097
A test throws four thousand random puts

159
00:06:10.097 --> 00:06:12.262
gets and removes at the map

160
00:06:12.337 --> 00:06:14.311
and compares every answer with

161
00:06:14.377 --> 00:06:16.167
Python's own dictionary.

162
00:06:16.517 --> 00:06:18.689
And the bad hash experiment

163
00:06:18.767 --> 00:06:21.017
seven hundred twenty anagrams in

164
00:06:21.087 --> 00:06:22.727
one thousand and nine buckets.

165
00:06:22.727 --> 00:06:24.646
With the sum hash

166
00:06:24.747 --> 00:06:26.543
every key lands in one chain

167
00:06:26.607 --> 00:06:27.951
of seven hundred twenty

168
00:06:28.007 --> 00:06:29.548
and one thousand and eight

169
00:06:29.607 --> 00:06:30.975
buckets stay empty.

170
00:06:31.047 --> 00:06:33.615
With the polynomial hash the

171
00:06:33.707 --> 00:06:35.237
longest chain is four.

172
00:06:35.817 --> 00:06:37.667
Get is the same walk.

173
00:06:37.667 --> 00:06:39.387
It finds the position in the chain

174
00:06:39.387 --> 00:06:40.867
and returns the value

175
00:06:40.867 --> 00:06:43.257
or a default if the key is missing.

176
00:06:43.607 --> 00:06:45.511
Because find also reports the

177
00:06:45.577 --> 00:06:46.777
number of comparisons

178
00:06:46.777 --> 00:06:49.557
the map can record them in a trace object

179
00:06:49.557 --> 00:06:51.277
and that is where the one

180
00:06:51.277 --> 00:06:53.727
three and three of the video came from.

181
00:06:54.307 --> 00:06:56.743
Remove finds the entry and

182
00:06:56.837 --> 00:06:58.467
pops it out of its chain.

183
00:06:58.817 --> 00:07:00.727
If the key is not there

184
00:07:00.727 --> 00:07:02.527
Python raises a key error

185
00:07:02.527 --> 00:07:04.187
like the built-in dictionary.

186
00:07:04.187 --> 00:07:07.257
The Java version returns null instead.

187
00:07:07.607 --> 00:07:09.417
Nothing else changes

188
00:07:09.417 --> 00:07:10.977
no other slot is touched

189
00:07:10.977 --> 00:07:13.057
and no entry has to move.

190
00:07:13.057 --> 00:07:15.737
That is the great simplicity of chaining

191
00:07:15.737 --> 00:07:18.407
which open addressing will not have.

192
00:07:18.987 --> 00:07:20.817
Two mistakes to avoid.

193
00:07:20.817 --> 00:07:24.587
Compare keys with equality, not identity

194
00:07:24.677 --> 00:07:26.506
two equal strings built at different

195
00:07:26.557 --> 00:07:28.276
times are different objects

196
00:07:28.337 --> 00:07:30.257
and a map that compared identity

197
00:07:30.317 --> 00:07:31.707
would not find them.

198
00:07:32.057 --> 00:07:34.769
And update, do not add

199
00:07:34.887 --> 00:07:36.736
putting a key that is already in the

200
00:07:36.787 --> 00:07:38.947
chain must replace its value.

201
00:07:38.947 --> 00:07:40.987
Otherwise the size grows

202
00:07:40.987 --> 00:07:43.537
and the old value hides behind the new one.

203
00:07:44.117 --> 00:07:47.287
To recap: a list of lists

204
00:07:47.287 --> 00:07:48.727
one chain per slot

205
00:07:48.727 --> 00:07:53.247
and every operation touches only one chain.

206
00:07:53.247 --> 00:07:55.867
Python's own dict does not use chaining

207
00:07:55.867 --> 00:07:57.997
as you will see in the next videos.

